{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":501621,"name":"Gurkirat Singh","username":"Gurkirat_Singh","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/g/c67d28/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-09-26T14:37:35.889Z","cooked":"\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-where-does-the-bug-appear-featureproduct-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-where-does-the-bug-appear-featureproduct-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eWhere does the bug appear (feature/product)?\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-describe-the-bug-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-describe-the-bug-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eDescribe the Bug\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot 0.59.1 opens and stays signed in, but it cannot reach the agent computer. The same failures start within about one second of every launch. Restarting does not help.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKaspersky Premium is scanning encrypted connections and presenting its own certificate. For \u003ccode\u003ehttps://api2.cursor.sh\u003c/code\u003e the issuer was:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eO=AO Kaspersky Lab, CN=Kaspersky Anti-Virus Personal Root Certificate\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSubject: \u003ccode\u003eCN=api2.cursor.sh\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot trusts only public CAs, so it drops the connection before the request is sent and logs \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e. Curl and the browser still work because they trust the Windows certificate store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhile retrying, one Grok Bot process held about 1,240 established HTTPS connections, then about 1,430, and the count was still rising.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLog evidence, 26 Sep 2026, about 19:22 and again at 19:39 (Asia/Calcutta):\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand-access\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003eentitlement-read\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecursor-auth\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003eprofile-fetch\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecursor-profile\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003eprivacy-mode\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eteams-membership\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eteam-policy-read\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ehard-limit\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ebox-host\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003erun-state\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.box_reachability\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eoutcome=network\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ecause=ControlPortCallError\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ebase_url_kind=unknown\u003c/code\u003e (\u003ccode\u003egetHostStatus\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003elistAgents\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003egetTrays\u003c/code\u003e)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.box_migration_watch\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003ephase=dropped\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eerror_class=Internal\u003c/code\u003e, consecutive failures past 187\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.box_setup_visible\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003etrigger=first_connect\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eoutcome=abandoned\u003c/code\u003e (about 1.8s)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.box_recreate_visible\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003etrigger=manual_reset\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eoutcome=failed\u003c/code\u003e (about 0.5s)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.box_secrets.push\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eerror_class=host_unreachable\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003esand.update.check\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eTypeError\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ecause=fetch_throw\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eerror_code=SAND-E0501\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003elocal-exec\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003ecredential-post\u003c/code\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eTypeError\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecrashSeen=false\u003c/code\u003e. No Crashpad dump.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-steps-to-reproduce-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-steps-to-reproduce-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eSteps to Reproduce\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eInstall Kaspersky Premium 21.26 on Windows. Leave encrypted-connection scanning at the default: \u003cstrong\u003eScan encrypted connections upon request from protection components\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eConfirm \u003ccode\u003eavp.exe\u003c/code\u003e is running.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eInstall and sign in to Grok Bot 0.59.1 (Stable).\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eLaunch Grok Bot.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWithin about one second, profile, entitlement, and agent-computer calls fail with \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e, and \u003ccode\u003ebase_url_kind\u003c/code\u003e stays \u003ccode\u003eunknown\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWatch the main \u003ccode\u003eGrok Bot.exe\u003c/code\u003e process. Established HTTPS connections climb past 1,000.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChecks that show the network itself is fine:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ehttps://grok.com\u003c/code\u003e returns HTTP 200.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecurl.exe --noproxy \"*\" -I https://test123.us10.cursorvm.com\u003c/code\u003e returns \u003ccode\u003eHTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eServer: awselb/2.0\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThe certificate for \u003ccode\u003ehttps://api2.cursor.sh\u003c/code\u003e is issued by Kaspersky’s personal root, not a public CA.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-expected-behavior-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-expected-behavior-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eExpected Behavior\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot should connect to the account API and the agent computer. If a local HTTPS scanner substitutes a non-public certificate, the app should report a certificate-trust error that names the issuer, and it should stop retrying instead of opening more than a thousand connections.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-operating-system-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-operating-system-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eOperating System\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWindows 10/11\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-version-information-6\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-version-information-6\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eVersion Information\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot:\u003cbr\u003e\nVersion: 0.59.1\u003cbr\u003e\nRelease Track: stable\u003cbr\u003e\nOS: win32 10.0.26300\u003cbr\u003e\nRuntime: Electron 42.1.0, Chrome 148.0.7778.97, Node 24.15.0\u003cbr\u003e\nInternal release: \u003ca href=\"mailto:sand@0.59.1\"\u003esand@0.59.1\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-for-ai-issues-which-model-did-you-use-7\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-for-ai-issues-which-model-did-you-use-7\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFor AI issues: which model did you use?\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNot an AI-model issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-for-ai-issues-add-request-id-with-privacy-disabled-8\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-for-ai-issues-add-request-id-with-privacy-disabled-8\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFor AI issues: add Request ID with privacy disabled\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNo request ID was issued. The calls failed locally before a request was sent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-additional-information-9\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-additional-information-9\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eAdditional Information\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCPU: AMD Ryzen 5 4600H. Time zone: Asia/Calcutta. Account was signed in.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eSystem proxy was off. Firewall profiles were on. No hosts-file overrides for Grok or x.ai.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThis matches the earlier 0.44.0 Kaspersky report. It still reproduces on 0.59.1.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWorkaround that worked: leave global scanning on. In Kaspersky, open \u003cstrong\u003eNetwork settings → Configure trusted applications\u003c/strong\u003e. Add \u003ccode\u003eC:\\Program Files\\Grok Bot\\Grok Bot.exe\u003c/code\u003e. Set \u003cstrong\u003eDo not scan all traffic\u003c/strong\u003e to \u003cstrong\u003eActive\u003c/strong\u003e. Fully quit Grok Bot and open it again.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eAfter that relaunch at 19:50 local: signed in, window maximized, \u003ccode\u003ecrashSeen=false\u003c/code\u003e, connections stayed around 7, the \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e spill log was not recreated, \u003ccode\u003esand-statsig-bootstrap.json\u003c/code\u003e refreshed (it had been stuck on 9 Sep 2026 because of \u003ccode\u003eSAND-E0501\u003c/code\u003e), and \u003ccode\u003egateway-descriptor.json\u003c/code\u003e was written at 19:51.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eTwo product gaps: \u003ccode\u003eConnectError.Internal\u003c/code\u003e does not say the TLS certificate is not publicly trusted, and the retry loop does not cap or close sockets.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-501621-does-this-stop-you-from-using-cursor-10\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-501621-does-this-stop-you-from-using-cursor-10\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eDoes this stop you from using Cursor\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNo - Cursor works, but with this issue\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-26T14:55:40.226Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":8,"reads":3,"readers_count":2,"score":40.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":173062,"topic_slug":"kaspersky-https-scanning-makes-grok-bot-0-59-1-on-windows-fail-every-api-call-with-connecterror-internal-and-leak-over-a-thousand-connections","display_username":"Gurkirat Singh","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":139890,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":false,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/kaspersky-https-scanning-makes-grok-bot-0-59-1-on-windows-fail-every-api-call-with-connecterror-internal-and-leak-over-a-thousand-connections/173062/1","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":501653,"name":"Colin","username":"Colin","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/forum.cursor.com/colin/{size}/90452_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-26T14:55:48.081Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eHey \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/gurkirat_singh\"\u003e@Gurkirat_Singh\u003c/a\u003e, thanks for the detailed report, and for sharing the workaround.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou’re right about the cause. Grok Bot currently checks server certificates against its own built-in list of public certificate authorities rather than the Windows certificate store, so when Kaspersky replaces the certificate for its encrypted-connection scanning, Grok Bot rejects the connection. The browser, curl, and Cursor all use the Windows store, which is why they keep working.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAdding Grok Bot.exe as a trusted application with “Do not scan all traffic” (or turning off encrypted-connection scanning) is the right fix for now, so please keep that exclusion in place.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’ve passed this to the team, including both points you raised: the error should say the certificate isn’t trusted and name the issuer, and the app shouldn’t keep retrying and opening new connections when this happens.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-26T14:55:48.081Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":3,"readers_count":2,"score":5.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":173062,"topic_slug":"kaspersky-https-scanning-makes-grok-bot-0-59-1-on-windows-fail-every-api-call-with-connecterror-internal-and-leak-over-a-thousand-connections","display_username":"Colin","primary_group_name":"CursorStaff","flair_name":"CursorStaff","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/cursor1/original/3X/a/d/ad08adc640929ae1e98be69dab1ad0844cee99a0.png","flair_bg_color":"","flair_color":"","flair_group_id":42,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"Community Support Engineer","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":87436,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":false,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/kaspersky-https-scanning-makes-grok-bot-0-59-1-on-windows-fail-every-api-call-with-connecterror-internal-and-leak-over-a-thousand-connections/173062/6","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null}],"stream":[501621,501653]},"timeline_lookup":[[1,0]],"suggested_topics":[],"tags":[{"id":325,"name":"windows","slug":"windows"},{"id":416,"name":"grok-bot","slug":"grok-bot"},{"id":408,"name":"networking","slug":"networking"}],"tags_descriptions":{},"fancy_title":"Kaspersky HTTPS scanning makes Grok Bot 0.59.1 on Windows fail every API call with ConnectError.Internal and leak over a thousand connections","id":173062,"title":"Kaspersky HTTPS scanning makes Grok Bot 0.59.1 on Windows fail every API call with ConnectError.Internal and leak over a thousand connections","posts_count":2,"created_at":"2026-09-26T14:37:35.808Z","views":23,"reply_count":0,"like_count":0,"last_posted_at":"2026-09-26T14:55:48.081Z","visible":true,"closed":false,"archived":false,"has_summary":false,"archetype":"regular","slug":"kaspersky-https-scanning-makes-grok-bot-0-59-1-on-windows-fail-every-api-call-with-connecterror-internal-and-leak-over-a-thousand-connections","category_id":6,"word_count":826,"deleted_at":null,"user_id":139890,"featured_link":null,"pinned_globally":false,"pinned_at":null,"pinned_until":null,"image_url":null,"slow_mode_seconds":0,"draft":null,"draft_key":"topic_173062","draft_sequence":null,"unpinned":null,"pinned":false,"current_post_number":1,"highest_post_number":6,"deleted_by":null,"actions_summary":[{"id":4,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":8,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":10,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":7,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false}],"chunk_size":20,"bookmarked":false,"topic_timer":null,"message_bus_last_id":26,"participant_count":2,"show_read_indicator":false,"thumbnails":null,"slow_mode_enabled_u